WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
The incident response plan is the umbrella document for the response program.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.460
It defines authority, scope, roles, severity model, communications expectations,

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.120
escalation paths, legal and privacy considerations,

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.780
and the broad lifecycle the organization follows.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.520
A playbook takes that plan and applies it to a specific scenario.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:26.020
The plan says how the organization responds. The playbook says how to respond when this

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.760
type of thing happens.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.820
Most incident response programs use a lifecycle that includes preparation,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:37.960
detection and analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, and post-incident improvement.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:43.540
The names may vary, but the flow is familiar. Preparation builds the people, tools,

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.640
access, logging, contacts, and documentation needed before an incident.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.420
Detection and analysis turn signals into an understanding of what may be happening.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:59.240
Containment limits damage. Eradication removes the cause, foothold, malicious artifact,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.300
or unauthorized access.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.720
Recovery restores normal operation safely. Post-incident improvement captures lessons

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.700
learned and updates controls.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:14.280
A playbook makes those stages concrete. In preparation, it lists required data sources,

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.620
access prerequisites, contact groups, ticket templates, and known dependencies.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.680
In detection and analysis,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.620
it defines triggering conditions and triage questions. In containment,

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:32.520
it lists options such as isolating a host, disabling an account, revoking tokens,

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.900
blocking an indicator, quarantining email, restricting a network path,

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.120
or pausing an exposed service.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.500
In eradication and recovery,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:47.000
it describes how to remove the unwanted condition and verify that the system is safe to

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.020
return.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:52.640
In post-incident improvement, it points to closure criteria, lessons learned,

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.140
control updates, and any required reporting.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:01.880
The playbook should also identify owners. During an incident, vague ownership wastes time.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.500
The SOC may own triage. IT may own endpoint actions.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.680
The identity team may own account controls. The cloud team may own audit logs and key

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:12.940
revocation.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:18.440
Legal or privacy teams may own notification analysis. Business owners may decide

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.100
acceptable downtime.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.200
Communications may prepare stakeholder updates. Executives may need concise risk

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:26.380
summaries.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.240
The playbook does not need every person's name forever,

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.460
but it should define the role or group responsible for each decision and action.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.760
Alignment matters. A playbook should fit the organization's escalation model,

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.580
legal obligations, communications process, and tooling.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.280
If the incident response plan requires legal review before external notifications,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.980
the playbook should not invent a separate path.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.040
If the organization uses a ticketing system as the record of action,

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.180
the playbook should name that record.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.360
If cloud audit logs are retained in a central system,

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:02.420
the playbook should point responders there rather than making them search from scratch.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.280
Think of the plan as the governance layer and the playbook as the operational layer.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.780
The playbook translates policy and lifecycle language into responder behavior: trigger,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:20.200
triage, classify, contain, eradicate, recover, document, communicate, close, and improve.
